   "A parse failure loses the seat's text; a validation failure keeps it. This run produced one of each, which is the cleanest possible demonstration. checks/spider_runner.clj:184 builds :value with (edn/read-string response) eagerly inside invoke-seat!, so a non-EDN reply throws there, before the {:wire ... :raw response ...} map is returned. invoke-valid! catches that as :invoke-error (checks/spider_runner.clj:334-336) and builds {:error ...} with no :raw and no :wire (:337-338), unlike the validation-failure branch at :339-343 which carries both. The receipt written at :347-350 therefore records :response nil :wire nil, and the retry prompt at :353-357 appends 'Your malformed output was:' followed by nothing. Observed: turn-009-malformed-1.edn (lp-norm-comparison, 'Invalid leading character: @') has :response nil :wire nil; turn-020-malformed-1.edn (series-evaluation-api, 'checkpoint turn requires one restatement') has both populated. Parse failures are the failure mode the retry loop exists for, and they are the one kind whose evidence is discarded. Reported, not fixed: changing the runner mid-wave is outside this row's acceptance.",
